# The actual highest-input / Whittaker-output boundary retains two walls Research proof, 27 October 2026. Fixing the highest INPUT column and retaining the original output row gives an exact source for every original root knot period. Its whole finite matrices admit a strict cyclic presentation with two separate endpoint defects. The selected pair sees a nonzero first-normal wrap class, so those defects cannot be discarded when proposing a boundary-torsor comparison. The classical Springer boundary map is explicit, but is only a candidate principal incidence for this source. A whole network and relative-endpoint map is not proved by its existence. In particular the one-sided uninserted Fourier polynomial is not a subcomplex for all actual port currents. ## 1. The genuine highest-input source and its remaining Fourier sum Use the scalar root closure and whole source homotopy in [the one-index theorem](actual_one_index_fourier_source_and_ward_reduction.md). With psi=e_0 and ell the all-ones ROW, the original root period is \[ P_N(M)=\lambda_0(C_N)=\ell C_N e_0. 	ag{1} \] There is no first average in (1); only the INPUT has been fixed. The output row still sums all c. For the literal two-cut tensor, at a=0 total-index conservation says c=-nu, hence \[ F_0(\eta_F)=\sum_{b,c,d}T_{c,d;0,b}\eta_F^{d-b} =\sum_{
u=-(N-1)}^0 f_
u\eta_F^
u. \] Its actual Fourier partial family and final source are exactly \[ \widetilde F_N(0,j)= rac1N\sum_{
u=-(N-1)}^0 Q_N^{j
u} f_
u,\qquad P_N=\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}\widetilde F_N(0,j)=f_0. 	ag{2} \] The 1/N inside (2) belongs to the inverse Fourier matrix. The original pivots, framing lines and complete d_N(M), if included, remain in T. Whole q-normal identities use the actual Vandermonde inverse and the source-current correction of the one-index theorem, not an off-root assertion that (1) is scalar for every auxiliary lift. The one-sided property applies to the charge-zero uninserted tensor. A homogeneous port-current tensor of total integer charge d instead has c+nu=d, so nu can range from d-(N-1) to d. This distinction matters even for ordinary generators; it is not a large-band issue. ## 2. Exact whole finite generators and boundary equations Work before jets with B=diag(1,Q,...,Q^(N-1)), J_head=diag(j), U e_j=e_(j+1) with its last arrow zero, and Uflat=U transpose. The actual balanced compressed matrices are \[ F=U(I-QB),\qquad \mathcal E=Uflat(MB^{-1}-M^{-1}I),\qquad K=xB^{-1},\quad x=-M/q,\quad q^2=Q. \] Put Z=Q^N, delta=1-Z, kappa=M-M inverse and define the constant cyclic repair and its two wrap arrows \[ P=U+W,\quad W=E_{0,N-1},\quad P^{-1}=Uflat+W_-,\quad W_-=E_{N-1,0},\quad P^N=I. \] Direct matrix multiplication proves the WHOLE identities \[ BP-QPB=\delta W, 	ag{3} \] \[ F=P(I-QB)-\delta W,\qquad \mathcal E=P^{-1}(MB^{-1}-M^{-1}I)-\kappa W_-. 	ag{4} \] Thus at the complete root delta=kappa=0 these are the literal cyclic quantum-torus formulas. Away from that fiber (3)-(4) retain the actual finite walls. P is a presentation device with the displayed defects; the actual U is still nilpotent. The right highest-input equations hold WHOLE: \[ B\psi=\psi,\qquad K\psi=x\psi,\qquad \mathcal E\psi=0,\qquad F\psi=(1-Q)e_1. 	ag{5} \] The output row obeys ell P=ell P inverse=ell and \[  oxed{\quad \ell F=\ell(I-QB)-\delta e_{N-1}^t,\qquad \ell\mathcal E=\ell(MB^{-1}-M^{-1}I)-\kappa e_0^t. \quad} 	ag{6} \] These are the actual Whittaker-type boundary equations. At the complete root their two endpoint rows vanish. Their first normals are independent: the Q-normal row is supported at the top head, and the M-normal row at the bottom. Neither may be discarded by replacing the source with a joint ordinary character beforehand. The source matrix Q_0=psi ell has trace one. The commuting left B action and right P action have the exact joint eigenspace \[ \{A:BA=A,\ AP=A\}=R Q_0. 	ag{7} \] Here invert Q^j-1 for 0<j<N, as in the actual head ring. BA=A forces only its zeroth row, and AP=A makes that row constant. Thus (7) is a whole pointed rank-one paired-source line. Left and right actions commute; it does NOT assert a one-dimensional character for the noncommutative algebra generated by B,P. ## 3. The selected pair detects the missing wrap at first normal order Define only for comparison the cyclic expressions \[ F_{cyc}=P(I-QB),\qquad \mathcal E_{cyc}=P^{-1}(MB^{-1}-M^{-1}I). \] The actual F is nilpotent, so F^N=0. One full cyclic traversal visits each head position once, giving the exact whole equation \[ F_{cyc}^N=(Q;Q)_N I =\delta (Q;Q)_{N-1}I. \] Consequently the ORIGINAL highest/Whittaker pair satisfies \[ \ell F^N\psi=0,\qquad \left[ rac{\ell F_{cyc}^N\psi}{1-Q^N}ight]_{Q_N}=N. 	ag{8} \] For the other wall, already one generator gives \[ \ell\mathcal E\psi=0,\qquad \ell\mathcal E_{cyc}\psi=\kappa. \] At the root its full cyclic central product is \[ \mathcal E_{cyc}^N=(-1)^{N-1}(M^N-M^{-N})I. 	ag{9} \] Away from the root the product is the original finite product product_(j=0)^(N-1)(M Q^(-j)-M inverse), rather than its root norm. This retains the same peripheral boundary and central factors as the original norm-root/modified-dimension construction. Their coalescing zeros must be evaluated as their whole ratios. There is a sharp algebraic obstruction to dropping these walls: an invertible B and P with exact BP=QPB and P^N=I would imply (1-Q^N)B=0. It cannot be a whole family over a ring where 1-Q^N is a genuine nonzero parameter and B remains invertible. Equation (3) is the required concrete finite correction. At fixed roots a cyclic quantum torus is legitimate; (8) shows why its bare root specialization loses a selected first-normal class. ## 4. A strict polynomial realization of the source before jets Identify the actual head with polynomials f(z) of degree below N, e_j corresponding to z^j. Then psi=1, ell(f)=f(1), and \[ Bf(z)=f(Qz),\quad \mathcal E f(z)= rac{M f(Q^{-1}z)-M^{-1}f(z)-\kappa f(0)}z, \] \[ Ff(z)=z[f(z)-Qf(Qz)]-\delta f_{N-1}z^N. 	ag{10} \] The first correction removes the actual north pole and the second removes the south degree overflow. Formula (10) is an exact global finite-section model on H^0(P1,O(N-1)), including its whole two endpoint operators. Its dual row is the explicit Serre cochain \[ c_\ell(z)=\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}z^{-j-1},\qquad \operatorname{Res}_{z=0}c_\ell(z)f(z)dz=f(1). 	ag{11} \] It therefore carries the actual source and all normal derivatives without replacing functions by their endpoint jets. To transport arbitrary products or relative word maps, carry the full operators and their endpoint rows, as in the established whole Cech model. ## 5. What its Springer boundary incidence does and does not supply The classical complete physical cut chart, with common momentum temporarily restored, is \[ s=A^{-1}-1,\quad \mu(A,p)= egin{pmatrix}1+s&-sp\s/p&1-s\end{pmatrix}, \quad v=p^{-1},\quad \eta_S=-sp. \] If a principal source transform carries the exact left/right characters B-input=1 and P-output=1 into these physical variables, its candidate corner is A=1,p=1, with eta_S=0 and the RETAINED Springer flag v=1. This is a conditional principal incidence, not an identification of the Fourier-band cap or of a uniform endpoint symbol map. The highest input is an endpoint of the original finite head, outside the previously proved regular good-A Gaussian chart. On the geometric side itself the associated centralizer resolution has the exact local map \[ v=1,\quad \eta_S=-s,\quad a_B=1-r,\quad b=(1-r)/s. \] The renormalized a_B is regular while the raw additive centralizer b has its genuine pole. Pulling back the actual geometric density gives \[ \Omega_{cut}=- rac{dr\wedge ds}{s(1+s)} =- rac{d\eta_S\wedge da_B}{\eta_S(1-\eta_S)},\qquad \operatorname{Res}_{\eta_S=0}\Omega_{cut}=-da_B=dr. 	ag{12} \] This is a ONE-form on the exceptional fiber v=1, not the full compact two-form or its already-computed compact period. It is exact on that affine fiber, so a nonzero relative value requires its actual endpoints and is their difference in r. The remaining finite Fourier-label sum must supply those data through a genuine source map; its existence is not inferred from (12). There is also a geometric obstruction to an ordinary regular raw matrix extension. For an irreducible complete representation some other word has nonzero lower-left entry c. In the actual r,s conjugator its appropriate entry has a c/s term; equivalently the nontrivial commutator trace cannot approach the identity trace while all matrices remain regular. Thus the endpoint comparison must use the resolved/meromorphic relative framing object. One Springer flag alone cannot regularize the full irreducible knot family. This is consistent with the actual moving walls (3)-(9), not their removal. ### 5.1. The actual second momentum is rational on a regular exceptional fiber There is a more precise geometric boundary chart, independent of an unproved quantum source map. Retain the ACTUAL signed prefixes U,V of the second physical edge and suppose c_V and c_U-c_V are nonzero. Let r differ from zero and one, and take s to zero with r fixed. The original expression k=ell_U/ell_V gives \[ k\longrightarrow k_0=c_U/c_V,\qquad C_0=U-k_0V= egin{pmatrix}a_0&b_0\0&d_0\end{pmatrix}, \qquad a_0d_0=(k_0-1)^2. \] Write C(s)=U-k(s)V. Its fiber equation ell_C=0 implies \[ c_C/s\longrightarrow- rac{d_0+(a_0-d_0)r}{r(1-r)}. \] For the actual F(r,s) conjugation the off-diagonal entries of F inverse C F are beta,gamma. Since its first diagonal is zero, direct multiplication now gives \[  eta\longrightarrow-d_0 rac{1-r}{r},\qquad \gamma\longrightarrow a_0 rac r{1-r}. \] The actual balanced momentum definition beta=(-1)^j(k-1)p_e therefore has the rational limit \[ p_e\longrightarrow\gamma_0 rac{1-r}{r},\qquad \gamma_0=- rac{(-1)^j d_0}{k_0-1}
e0. 	ag{12a} \] Thus the remaining momentum is birational on this regular part of the exceptional fiber, with inverse r=gamma_0/(p_e+gamma_0). The boundary density is exactly \[ dr=- rac{\gamma_0}{(p_e+\gamma_0)^2}dp_e. 	ag{12b} \] Its genuine pole is at p_e=-gamma_0 and its ordinary logarithmic residue is zero. A relative endpoint pairing cannot be inferred by discarding that pole. The raw representation escape and the local regularity of this particular physical Gauss coordinate are distinct. For the genuine figure-eight third prefix in [the prefix theorem](prefix_adapted_two_position_framing_cover.md), u squared plus u plus one is zero and k_0=2+u, a_0=-1, d_0=-u, gamma_0=u squared. All conditions of this chart hold. Equation (12a) specifies a candidate coordinate for the last Fourier-label source at the resolved boundary. The whole finite sum, its possible passage through the pole in (12b), the internal crossing coefficients and moving normal factors must still be transported before it is a selected period identity. ## 6. One-sided scalar bands are not a current subcomplex The literal two-cut unknot is the flip tensor. Fix a=0 and insert the actual second OUTPUT F generator. The input b=0 term has c=0,d=1 and coefficient 1-Q. It contributes \[ (1-Q)\eta_F^{+1}, 	ag{13} \] which is nonzero at every physical N greater than one. Thus even this actual port current exits the nonpositive-band subspace in (2). It must retain its positive tail in the whole Fourier/current complex. This statement concerns a literal port row, not an assumed untwisted two-leg coproduct on the reshaped tensor. Actual total Hopf rows still use their correct ordered port/prefix dictionary. ## 7. Exact audit and remaining map [The audit](../experiments/audit_highest_whittaker_boundary.py) and [its output](../experiments/highest_whittaker_boundary_results.json) check whole matrix boundary formulas, both cyclic norms and their selected wrap normal, actual signed R-word highest-input Fourier sources, and the positive current tail (13). The strongest exact conclusion is the whole source line, polynomial realization and its retained normal endpoints, together with a concrete candidate geometric relative fiber. It does not construct the internal R/Gaussian transform to that fiber, prove nonzero selected geometric survival, or determine the hyperbolic-volume growth. Such a transform must preserve (6), (8), (11), the full peripheral zero ratios and every current tail, not only (12).

# Irreducible critical sources force nonzero conjugate coefficients Research theorem, 27 October 2026. A fixed whole algebraic critical source can provide more than uniqueness of a proposed comparison: Galois descent can force its geometric coefficient to be nonzero. The required source density is recovered from finitely many exact moments. A fixed algebraic presentation also gives polynomial bounds on every coefficient along the actual cyclotomic source arc, even though the degrees of the specialized cyclotomic fields grow. These results supply a finite target for an action-normalized selected period comparison. They do not construct that comparison from the actual quantum state. In particular, algebraicity of Rivin's critical shapes alone does not prove the hypotheses below or the volume norm. ## 1. A descended source cannot kill one embedding of a field Let F be a characteristic-zero field and let A/F be a finite field extension of degree d. Let P be a rank-one A-line, with a source \(\psi\in P\) and an F-linear co-source \(\lambda:P	o F\). There is a unique A-linear map \(\eta:P	o A\) such that \[ \lambda=\operatorname{Tr}_{A/F}\circ\eta. 	ag{1.1} \] Indeed choose a generator p of P. The trace pairing on A is nondegenerate, so \(x\mapsto\lambda(px)\) is uniquely \(x\mapsto\operatorname{Tr}(bx)\) for some b. Put \(\eta(px)=bx\). To prove nondegeneracy directly, choose a primitive element theta with distinct conjugates \(	heta_i\). The trace Gram matrix in \(1,	heta,\ldots,	heta^{d-1}\) is \(V^tV\), where \(V_{ij}=	heta_i^j\). Its determinant is the nonzero squared Vandermonde product. Put \(a=\eta(\psi)\). After splitting A over an algebraic closure, the source coefficient at its i-th primitive idempotent is exactly \[ \lambda(e_i\psi)=\sigma_i(a). 	ag{1.2} \] Thus if \(\psi
e0\) and \(\lambda
e0\), every coefficient in (1.2) is nonzero. The field assumption matters: multiplication by two nonzero elements cannot give zero. A particularly simple exact certificate is \[ \lambda(\psi)=1, 	ag{1.3} \] since \(\operatorname{Tr}(a)=1\) forces \(a
e0\). Changing the generator of P changes b and the coordinate of psi inversely, and leaves a unchanged. A Gaussian determinant line or its marked algebraic density can therefore be retained as P; no arbitrary scalar trivialization is required. Multiplication by a fixed nonzero algebraic Jacobian or density preserves nonvanishing. For an A-module P of higher finite rank, (1.1) still holds by an A-basis. The certificate (1.3) still implies every coefficient of the paired element \(a=\eta(\psi)\) is nonzero. Merely assuming that psi and lambda are individually nonzero is sufficient only in rank one, where their product cannot vanish. This proves a useful replacement for an independently assumed nonzero geometric weight. It requires the ACTUAL source and co-source to descend to this irreducible algebra. A nonzero pairing elsewhere in a larger critical algebra does not prove (1.3) on its geometric component. ## 2. The source density is an exact finite moment calculation Write \(A=F[	heta]\), with a specified irreducible separable polynomial p of degree d. Suppose the actual proposed source descent supplies the finite moments \[ m_j=\lambda(	heta^j\psi),\quad 0\le j<d,\qquad G_{ij}=\operatorname{Tr}_{A/F}(	heta^{i+j}). \] Then the density \(a=\sum_{i=0}^{d-1}a_i	heta^i\) is determined by \[ G(a_i)_i=(m_j)_j. 	ag{2.1} \] The same nonzero Vandermonde determinant makes G invertible. Thus a whole period comparison need not guess its co-source density: these d exact moments construct its only possible density. In particular \(a
e0\) if and only if the moment vector is nonzero, and \(m_0=1\) is a direct certificate. If a proposed critical observable Theta acts on a finite F-space V, has irreducible characteristic polynomial of degree \(\dim_FV=d\), and the source psi is nonzero, then \(V=A\psi\). Indeed A is a field of dimension d and the map \(A	o V\), \(x\mapsto x\psi\), is injective. It is therefore an isomorphism. In this situation (2.1) uses actual moments \(\lambda(\Theta^j\psi)\). Any operator commuting with Theta is multiplication by an element of A, and the selected scalar is its trace against a. The same statement holds after a specified period-ring extension, for an action operator in that commutant. To apply this to the knot, one must construct Theta or the critical line on an ACTUAL source quotient and prove the moment descent. The gluing critical field by itself is not such an operator. Neither an abstract isomorphism of Borel groups nor the existence of a local Gaussian block supplies these finite moments. ## 3. Exact normalized traces give another nonvanishing certificate Irreducibility can be replaced by a stronger trace realization. Let A be any finite etale F-algebra, faithfully represented on V, and let \(\lambda(x)=\operatorname{Tr}_V(x)/\dim_FV\), with source the identity. After splitting A, each primitive idempotent e_i is a nonzero projection. Thus \[ \lambda(e_i)= rac{\operatorname{rank}e_i}{\dim_FV} 
e0. 	ag{3.1} \] No positivity, self-adjointness, or irreducibility is needed: an idempotent has eigenvalues zero and one, and its trace is its rank. Equivalently, for \(A=\prod K_j\), a faithful A-module has positive integer multiplicities \(r_j\), and its trace density on K_j is \(r_j/\dim_FV\). Even if \(\dim V_N\le C N^b\) grows, the weights in (3.1) lie between \(C^{-1}N^{-b}\) and one. They cannot exponentially suppress a geometric sector. Multiplying by a fixed algebraic Gaussian density which is a UNIT on the selected etale algebra preserves this conclusion by Section 4. Nondegeneracy must hold on each selected component; a nonzero density on only one factor of a product would not suffice. The actual compact cut is a normalized matrix trace, but this does not by itself establish (3.1) for a geometric critical sector. One needs an exact trace-compatible critical summand of the actual transfer or relative period object. Embedding an unrelated critical algebra into a color matrix algebra is insufficient. In particular, the already closed one-cut knot matrix is scalar; projecting that matrix repeats the SAME scalar and does not decompose its phases. ## 4. A fixed whole presentation gives polynomial bounds on the root arc Use the root SOURCE slice, after fixing its actual finite phases and power coordinates \(Q^N=1\), \(M=1\), \(M^N=1\). On this slice put \[ F=k(t,Q),\quad k\subset\mathbb C,\qquad (t,Q)=\gamma(1/N) =(1/N,\exp(c/N)),\quad c=2\pi i. 	ag{4.1} \] Fix the displayed embedding of k in the complex numbers. The following statement concerns this slice, not the larger space in which \(Q^N\) is an independent quantum-normal coordinate. Any fixed residue class of levels needed for a marked finite phase can be used. All constants below are uniform along that class. **Polynomial bound theorem.** Let a be a nonzero element of a fixed finite field extension A/F. For every conjugate value a_(i,N) of its specialization on (4.1), all sufficiently large N satisfy \[ C^{-1}N^{-B}\le |a_{(i,N)}| \le C N^B. 	ag{4.2} \] The constants depend on the fixed whole algebraic presentation, not on N or the chosen conjugate. No bounded degree of the specialized absolute cyclotomic fields, height assumption, or coherent choice of one analytic branch is needed. Here is an elementary proof. For any nonzero polynomial \[ R(t,Q)=\sum_j p_j(t)Q^{n_j},\qquad n_j	ext{ distinct}, \] the pullback \(R(t,e^{ct})=\sum_jp_j(t)e^{cn_jt}\) is nonzero. To see linear independence, isolate one exponential by applying \(\prod_{k
e j}(\partial_t-cn_k)^{\deg p_k+1}\). On its polynomial factor the remaining operators are \(\partial_t+c(n_j-n_k)\), each invertible on a bounded polynomial space, so a nonzero p_j cannot be killed. If \(m=\sum_j(\deg p_j+1)\), the entire sum solves the constant coefficient equation \(\prod_j(\partial_t-cn_j)^{\deg p_j+1}f=0\) of order m. Its coefficient recursion shows that a zero of order m would force all Taylor coefficients to vanish. Therefore \[ \operatorname{ord}_{t=0}R(t,e^{ct})\le m-1. 	ag{4.3} \] This also proves Zariski density of the arc (4.1) on this two-variable source slice. After clearing Laurent powers it applies to every nonzero rational numerator or denominator on that slice. Choose a polynomial relation for a over F, clear denominators and write it as \(\sum_{j=0}^dR_j(t,Q)a^j=0\), with both \(R_0\) and \(R_d\) nonzero. Such a relation exists with nonzero constant term because a is nonzero in a field. On the arc every nonzero coefficient has a finite order at zero by (4.3). Hence for small positive t, \(|R_d|\ge c_1t^{
u_d}\), \(|R_0|\ge c_1t^{
u_0}\), while all coefficients are bounded above. The elementary Cauchy root bound applied to this polynomial gives \(|a_i|\le C t^{-
u_d}\). Applied to its reciprocal polynomial it gives \(|a_i|\ge C^{-1}t^{
u_0}\). This proves (4.2), uniformly over all its roots. Formula (4.3) gives explicit exponents from the fixed coefficient supports. Apply this to the source density a in Section 1. Irreducible descent and the retained nonzero normalization force ALL geometric conjugate coefficients to be eventually nonzero and subexponential. This does not assume their nonvanishing separately. ## 5. Height hypotheses: what suffices and what does not There is also a simple arithmetic criterion, independent of degree. If a nonzero algebraic number alpha satisfies some integer polynomial whose coefficient maximum is H, then \[ (1+H)^{-1}\le|\alpha|\le1+H. 	ag{5.1} \] Remove zero constant powers first. The leading and constant integer coefficients then have absolute value at least one. For \(|z|>1+H\), the sum of the lower terms is smaller than the leading term by the geometric-series estimate \(H/(|z|-1)<1\); this proves the upper bound. Reverse the polynomial for the lower bound. Consequently \(\log H_N=o(N)\) forces \(\log|\alpha_N|=o(N)\), with NO condition on the polynomial degrees. This strengthens the sufficient degree-and-height estimate in the [earlier global function audit](global_period_rigidity_function_audit.md). H here is the naive coefficient height of an absolute integer annihilating polynomial, not the absolute logarithmic Weil height. Bounded Weil height without a degree bound is insufficient. For N>=2, the polynomial \[ z^N-2^Nz^{N-1}-2 	ag{5.2} \] is irreducible by the elementary Eisenstein argument at two: reducing a hypothetical monic integer factorization modulo two gives powers of z, so both constant terms would be even and their product would be divisible by four. It has one real root alpha_N in \((2^N,2^N+1)\). Every root with modulus at least one lies in \(|z-2^N|\le2\), since \(z^{N-1}(z-2^N)=2\). On that disk, \(z\mapsto2^N+2z^{-(N-1)}\) is a strict contraction, so it has at most one root there. Thus every other conjugate has modulus less than one. The Weil height is \(\log\alpha_N/N\), bounded and tending to log two, while \(N^{-1}\log|\alpha_N|	o\log2
e0\). Bounded defining height of the TORSOR alone says even less: a fixed \(\mathbb G_m\) torsor contains the points \(2^N\). The whole section or its selected-point height must be controlled. The fixed algebraic section theorem in Section 4 is one way to obtain control which does not require estimating absolute cyclotomic polynomial heights. ## 6. Two sharp limitations of irreducibility and normalization First, a split algebra can kill the geometric point. For \(A=F	imes F\), source \((1,1)\), and co-source \(\lambda(x,y)=y\), the normalization is one but the first primitive coefficient is zero. The same failure can occur if a co-source is allowed coefficients in a splitting field rather than descending to F. Thus levelwise algebraicity over \(\mathbb Q(\zeta_N)\) is not the whole descent assumption in Section 1. A fixed shape field can intersect these cyclotomic fields and split after such a base change. The theorem requires one whole descended co-source before these specializations, or the faithful trace realization of Section 3. Second, even field descent and normalization do not bound a merely levelwise source. In \(A=\mathbb Q(\sqrt2)\), take \[ a_N=(1+2^N\sqrt2)/2, \operatorname{Tr}(a_N)=1. 	ag{6.1} \] Both coefficients are nonzero but have exponential size. Multiplying a geometric exponential by either changes its rate by log two. The fixed quadratic field, its discriminant, and the normalization remain unchanged. The selected point has exponentially growing naive height; (6.1) is not a fixed algebraic function of \((t,Q)\) on (4.1). This separates finite-type geometry from bounded complexity of its selected section. ## 7. The resulting action-normalized period criterion Suppose an ACTUAL scalar period has been represented, with its source/cut preserved, in a finite global period space as \[ P_N=\sum_{i=1}^{d} a_{(i,N)}G_{(i,N)}. 	ag{7.1} \] Assume its source density is the fixed whole element of Sections 1--4, or its coefficients have the faithful trace weights of Section 3 times a fixed algebraic density which is a unit on every selected component. Suppose a specified geometric index g has \[  rac1N\log|G_{(g,N)}|\longrightarrow s_g,\qquad |G_{(i,N)}|\le\exp(N(s_g-\delta))\quad(i
e g) 	ag{7.2} \] for some positive delta and all sufficiently large N. The polynomial bounds and nonvanishing already proved imply \[  rac1N\log|P_N|\longrightarrow s_g. 	ag{7.3} \] Indeed the total of the other finitely many terms divided by the geometric term tends to zero exponentially, up to polynomial factors. This proves both the upper and lower rate, including cancellation control. It does not assume nonvanishing of the geometric coefficient as an extra hypothesis. If (7.1) is instead a proposed leading-block formula with a remainder, the TOTAL remainder requires a smaller-rate bound as well. Strict Rivin concavity on the positive real angle domain does not bound all other complex critical sectors or supply that global estimate. For the knot, the marked regulator gives \(s_g=\operatorname{Vol}/(2\pi)\) on the geometric period. The unresolved input is the source-preserving global period-line realization (7.1), together with (7.2), not another Taylor coefficient at the unique algebraic optimizer. The moment formula (2.1) and trace criterion (3.1) are exact, finite algebraic certificates for the missing source part of such a realization. This is separate from [actual Mellin root reconstruction](mellin_cyclotomic_jet_uniqueness.md): that theorem fixes the whole root function once the actual bounded quantum source jets and pole lattice are supplied. It does not yet construct the action-normalized critical trace (7.1). Nor does the present result replace the ACTUAL generally transcendental selected Mellin section by an algebraic function; the [adequate-knot transcendence theorem](adequate_mellin_transcendence.md) already excludes that replacement. The complementary [whole-unit weight theorem](whole_torsor_unit_weights_and_irregular_type.md) shows why finite type of the torsor CONNECTION is weaker than the bounded algebraic coefficient hypothesis in Section 4: a growing character of one fixed regular algebraic unit can carry a nonzero normal action. Its marked connection and character must be retained, even when the unit has classical value one. [Exact diagnostics](../experiments/audit_irreducible_critical_source.py). [Results](../experiments/irreducible_critical_source_results.json). [Independent proof audit](doubled_phase_and_critical_source_independent_audit.md). [Independent proof audit](doubled_phase_and_critical_source_independent_audit.md). The subsequent [actual pointed-commutant theorem](actual_pointed_commutant_and_critical_observable.md) rules out obtaining this irreducible critical observable as a whole horizontal endomorphism of the original pointed Mellin lattice. The [dual-clock theorem](dual_clock_normal_cut_source.md) also shows that raw framing moments have infinite Hilbert-matrix rank; they are not the isolated transverse critical algebra. A finite list of proposed moments constructs a density only AFTER the critical polynomial action or quotient has been supplied and verified. These tests do not infer critical descent from a finite moment fit.

